--- body ---

## Context

`verification/SITTER-LIFECYCLE-AUDIT-2026-09-12.md` audits the sitter against
its four contracts with the host: install, remove, update, and surviving a
Zotero upgrade or downgrade. Three of its ten findings cost a few lines each and
are implemented here; the rest are filed as 0772-0779.

**F9 — the journal records why the plugin stopped and never why it started.**
`shutdown(data, reason)` has named its reason since 0689. `startup()` took no
second argument at all, so the ring could say the add-on had been disabled,
uninstalled or upgraded, and never that *this* activation was the install, the
enable or the upgrade. That count — how many replacements one session has
taken — is exactly what 0727's one surviving live candidate is about.

**A fourth defect, found by the test written for F9 and fixed with it.** The
seal was lifted forty lines below the startup emit, so a seal still standing
from the previous `shutdown()` swallowed the startup record on every activation
after the first — in both channels. The first activation is not sealed and has
no ring yet, so its record reached the debug log alone; every later one reached
nothing. bootstrap.js's own comment at that call site asserted the opposite.
The ring therefore never held a startup record at all, which is the same defect
family as 0688's unreadable version: instrumentation built to say which
transition happened, not saying it.

**F3 — two session globals outlive an uninstall.** `Zotero.SDTPackSitterJournal`
was republished unconditionally and `Zotero.SDTPackSitterCacheWrite` was never
deleted. Both are right for the reason they were built (0703: a disable must not
destroy the evidence of what it is recovering from; the write chain is shared
across scopes so a replacement's first write cannot interleave with the outgoing
build's last). Neither reason survives an uninstall, which has no next
activation to read the ring, no window left to copy it from, and no successor to
serialize against.

**F7 — the cache's format guard could not fire.** `scheduler.js`'s
`raw?.format === 1` compares against the literal `bootstrap.js` had just handed
it: satisfied by construction, unable ever to fire, and reading as protection
the cache did not have. No row on disk carried a stamp, so an older build
installed over a newer one — ordinary for a plugin delivered by hand — read the
newer build's records as its own.

## Relevant files

- `plugins/sdt-sitter/bootstrap.js` — all three changes
- `tests/sdt_sitter_bootstrap.mjs` — five new scenarios, over the real `startup()`
- `tests/sdt_sitter_zotero_mock.mjs` — `CACHE_FORMAT`, and `cacheLine` stamps it
- `verification/probes/sdt_sitter_bootstrap_mutants.py` — M40-M44; M4's anchor moved
- `plugins/sdt-sitter/manifest.json`, `update.json` — 0.3.45

## Actions

1. `BOOTSTRAP_REASONS` (was `SHUTDOWN_REASONS`, plus `1: 'app-startup'`) and one
   `nameBootstrapReason()` read by both directions.
2. `startup({ rootURI, version }, reason)`; the named reason rides the `startup`
   record, not `environment` — the environment is which build is running, the
   reason is what this activation was.
3. Lift the seal at the top of `initialize()`, ahead of the startup emit and
   deliberately NOT behind the generation token: a startup that never reaches
   the guard is exactly what the record exists to capture.
4. On reason `uninstall` alone, delete both session globals instead of
   republishing the ring.
5. Stamp every cache row with `raw.format` and require it on the way in. The
   number stays on the one line that already held it.

## Test

Five scenarios in `tests/sdt_sitter_bootstrap.mjs`, each red before its change:

- the startup record names the host's reason, and the ring reads as a sequence
  of transitions (`shutdown:disable`, `startup:enable`, `shutdown:upgrade`)
- a named reason (`ADDON_INSTALL`), an unknown number (`reason-9`) and no reason
  at all (`unknown`) are all readable
- an uninstall takes both globals; a disable keeps both (0703's positive control)
- a row stamped with another schema number is skipped and its pack re-opened
- the rows this build writes are the rows the next session reads (round trip)

## Verification

- [x] `node tests/sdt_sitter_bootstrap.mjs` — 59 scenarios pass
- [x] `node tests/sdt_sitter_scheduler.mjs`, `node tests/sdt_sitter_dialog.mjs`
- [x] `python3 verification/probes/sdt_sitter_bootstrap_mutants.py` — M40-M44 caught
- [x] `python3 bench/check_sitter_version.py` — 0.3.45 over 106 revisions
- [x] `make check`

## Invariants

- 0703's rule holds on every reason but `uninstall`.
- The ring stays volatile: nothing durable is added here.
- No new user-facing string, so the UI-string guards are untouched.
- An existing on-disk cache written by 0.3.43 carries no stamp and is dropped
  once, on the first activation of this build. The cache is disposable by
  ratification; 0774 is the ticket that makes such a drop legible on screen.

## Exit criteria

- The ring can be read as a sequence of transitions in both directions.
- An uninstalled add-on leaves neither global behind.
- A cache row's schema number is written and read, so a foreign row is skipped.
